narrative:
## Pattern Matching with Match

When you need different behavior for each enum variant, `match` is the right tool. It's like `if/elif` but designed specifically for enums.

### The Direction Enum

```xiom
enum Direction {
  North,
  South,
  East,
  West,
}
```

### Matching on Direction

```xiom
fn go(dir: Direction) -> Str {
  match dir {
    North => { return "You see a snowy mountain."; }
    South => { return "You see a sunny beach."; }
    East => { return "You see a dense forest."; }
    West => { return "You see a vast desert."; }
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(go(Direction.North));
  io.println(go(Direction.South));
  return 0;
}
```

### Match as an Expression

Match can return a value directly — no need for `return` inside each arm:

```xiom
fn describe(dir: Direction) -> Str {
  return match dir {
    North => "Cold",
    South => "Warm",
    East => "Mild",
    West => "Dry",
  };
}
```

### Exhaustiveness: Every Variant Required

The compiler checks that ALL variants are covered. This won't compile:

```xiom
match dir {
  North => { return "Mountains"; }
  South => { return "Beach"; }
  // ERROR: East and West not handled!
}
```

### Combining Variants

If multiple variants should do the same thing, use `|`:

```xiom
fn is_north_south(dir: Direction) -> Bool {
  return match dir {
    North | South => true,
    East | West => false,
  };
}
```

Match is the cleanest way to handle enums — one block, one arm per variant, no accidentally skipped cases.

